A||||11||dennis_6||Stuck plug!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||dennis_6||12-08-2001||07:40 PM||||I got my 86 SE (v6 auto) starting sorta.  Timing was off and the negative battery cable was shot.  The cable literally started to smoke.  <P>My problem is the plug closest to the driver (I think it is number 6) is stuck.  The tip broke off and I can feel the nut around the plug.  <P>I have tried every deepwell socket I own in addition to the standard 5/8.  Nothing will grip the nut.  I am at a complete loss of what to do.  <P>I really don't want to pull the head right now.  Is there anything else I can do?  Thanks.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by dennis_6 (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||12-08-2001||08:28 PM||||If you can feel the shell of the plug and it's still got (some semblance of) corners, you might try this: Soak in Liquid Wrench, use a "best fitting" socket from a cheap Taiwanese set (or whatever - the $5 socket sets).  Try both SAE and metric sizes, and get one that almost fits but is too small.  Pound the "almost fits" socket onto the stuck plug.  If you're lucky, it'll "broach" a tight-fitting hole in the cheapie socket.<P>Now use a flex-head ratchet or breaker bar on it, and a big cheater bar.  Don't apply too much force, rather, use a bigger cheater.  That way, you'll have better control and won't hurt yourself (as much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) if something slips.  After the threads break loose, just spin it out with a ratchet and throw the socket + plug away.  Or, keep it as a souvenier of this thrillling adventure   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>I used this method to get a badly-seized plug out of a Fiero V6.  The plug was seized because the exhaust manifold had a leak that had been spewing hot exhaust gas on that plug for who-knows-how-long.  Needless to say, the plug boot was toast, too.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    ||||reg||3
Z||000002||dennis_6||12-08-2001||08:39 PM||||Thanks - Stu Good.  I only hope I can hit the socket with something.  I have the plenum removed but it still really cramped back there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||12-08-2001||09:21 PM||||What the hell? You can still feel the hex, but nothing fits? Sounds like a chunk of ceramic might be wedged between the plug and the block. Grab a scratchall or a flathead screwdriver, and ensure that the area AROUND the plug is gunked up with shattered bits of plug... Other than that, it'll take a while to pound the socket on, but you can tilt the engine a little for more clearance. <BR>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||dennis_6||12-09-2001||03:53 AM||||Mach10 - thanks for the info.  <P>Nothing seems to be lodged and I can not get a socket to grip.  I am going to try less conventional warfare.   Can anyone say glue?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"><BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Banzai||12-09-2001||08:10 AM||||Don't forget the anti-sieze when you put the new plugs in.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||starfighter007@msn.com||12-09-2001||11:50 AM||||Dennis if you just broke the ceramic and the hex part of the plug is still there YOU HAD better stop what you are doing and get some one else to help you before you wreck what you have left of that plug and will have to pull the head at the very least.||||reg||4
Z||000007||avengador1||12-09-2001||12:14 PM||||Get a mirror and a flashlight. Assess the condition of what is left of the plug. If it looks good apply liberal amounts of penetrating oil like PB Buster or kroil to soak the threads. Make sure you use the right size socket or you will round the plug and have to remove the head then. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||dennis_6||12-09-2001||01:06 PM||||Starfighter007 - The ceramic part would not have broke if the previous owners had not helped the plug alone.  There was even a nut around the cermaic part and I am not talking about the 5/8 nut at the base.  The nut on the plug is stripped beyond any means of getting a socket on it.  The cermaic part was very loose and pretty much fell off when the socket slipped.||||reg||1
Z||000009||85 gt fiero||12-09-2001||03:56 PM||||take the dog bone off and pull the engine back you might have to take the decklid off and try to get one of those gator sockets that fit any nut then try to get the plug out thats what my friend did, worked fine for him expect it was a talon not a fiero.  Good luck<P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES, LIGHT BARS, SCS SUSPENSION, KONI ADJUSTERS,HIGH POINT CUTOFFS, AND BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||12-09-2001||06:21 PM||||I will "second" the Liquid Wrench suggestion.<BR>Let it marinate in it. The longer the better.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||cadaver||12-11-2001||01:07 AM||||Last one I had break I used mouse milk a torch to heat the head around the plug and a big easy out. It came out with no damage to the head.||||reg||1
